1
00:00:01,051 --> 00:00:08,772
Welcome to the Europe capitals, episode three of six. First, I clear the
chosen names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,430 --> 00:00:20,947
Nine capitals fill the crowded middle of Europe: the three of the Low
Countries and France, then Germany, the Czech Republic, Austria, Slovakia and
Hungary.

3
00:00:21,614 --> 00:00:24,122
Let's bring Amsterdam into view.

4
00:00:28,302 --> 00:00:34,780
Amsterdam lies in the north-west of the Netherlands, on the Amstel river where
it reaches the IJ.

5
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:44,136
"Dam on the Amstel" — a dam built about 1270, which is literally where the
city and its name began.

6
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:58,279
Remember Amsterdam as a city built on millions of wooden piles, with 165
canals dug in the 1600s and the first company to issue tradable shares founded
there in 1602.

7
00:00:58,976 --> 00:01:01,101
Let's bring Brussels into view.

8
00:01:04,868 --> 00:01:10,499
Brussels lies in the middle of Belgium, in the Flemish Brabant on the Senne
river.

9
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:19,217
From Bruocsella, "settlement in the marsh", the main institutions of the
European Union and the headquarters of NATO are here.

10
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:29,052
Remember Brussels as the settlement in the marsh that became the seat of the
European Union, with a fountain boy in more than a thousand costumes.

11
00:01:29,744 --> 00:01:32,426
Let's bring Luxembourg into view.

12
00:01:36,798 --> 00:01:44,937
Luxembourg stands on the Alzette river in the south-east of the country, where
it folds into Germany and France.

13
00:01:45,595 --> 00:01:51,481
Its fortress made it the "Gibraltar of the North", and its old town is a
UNESCO site.

14
00:01:52,139 --> 00:02:04,086
Remember Luxembourg as the Gibraltar of the North, with about 17 kilometres of
casemate tunnels cut into the rock and one of the three official seats of the
European Union.

15
00:02:04,776 --> 00:02:06,738
Let's bring Paris into view.

16
00:02:11,151 --> 00:02:20,915
Paris stands on the Seine in the north of France, on the plain of the Paris
basin some three hundred kilometres from the sea.

17
00:02:21,572 --> 00:02:28,689
From the Parisii, the Gaulish tribe on the Île de la Cité. The Romans called
it Lutetia.

18
00:02:29,342 --> 00:02:39,791
Remember Paris as the city of the Parisii and of Lutetia, whose catacombs hold
about six million people moved underground in 1786.

19
00:02:40,470 --> 00:02:42,595
Let's bring Berlin into view.

20
00:02:46,416 --> 00:02:54,601
Berlin lies in the north-east of Germany, on the Spree river, in the middle of
the great European plain.

21
00:02:55,258 --> 00:03:05,958
Not the bear: it is most likely Slavic berl-, a swamp. The bear on the coat of
arms is a pun that came later and stuck.

22
00:03:06,616 --> 00:03:14,209
Remember Berlin as the old swamp behind the bear, and the city the Wall ran
through for twenty-eight years.

23
00:03:14,907 --> 00:03:17,125
Let's bring Prague into view.

24
00:03:21,163 --> 00:03:27,386
Prague stands on the Vltava river in the centre of the Czech Republic, in the
landlocked heart of Europe.

25
00:03:28,037 --> 00:03:34,388
Praha probably comes from an old word for a threshold, or a ford across the
Vltava.

26
00:03:35,047 --> 00:03:45,194
Remember Prague as the castle complex often called the largest in the world,
and the astronomical clock of 1410 that still works.

27
00:03:45,884 --> 00:03:47,881
Let's bring Vienna into view.

28
00:03:51,884 --> 00:03:58,362
Vienna, called Wien in German, lies on the Danube in the east of Austria, at
the foot of the Alps.

29
00:03:59,024 --> 00:04:12,538
Named for the Wien river. The Romans had a camp here called Vindobona, and the
Congress of Vienna in 1814 and 1815 redrew the map of Europe after Napoleon.

30
00:04:13,193 --> 00:04:22,748
Remember Vienna as the camp of Vindobona, the city whose congress redrew
Europe, and whose coffee houses are on UNESCO's list of intangible heritage.

31
00:04:23,431 --> 00:04:26,450
Let's bring Bratislava into view.

32
00:04:30,116 --> 00:04:36,084
Bratislava stands on the Danube in the south-west of Slovakia, where three
countries meet.

33
00:04:36,748 --> 00:04:45,955
It took its present name only in 1919; before, it was Pressburg in German and
Pozsony in Hungarian.

34
00:04:46,612 --> 00:04:55,528
Remember Bratislava as the only capital that borders two other countries,
Austria and Hungary, with its white castle over the Danube.

35
00:04:56,212 --> 00:04:58,209
Let's bring Budapest into view.

36
00:05:02,297 --> 00:05:07,371
Budapest straddles the Danube in central Hungary, on the Pannonian plain.

37
00:05:08,030 --> 00:05:21,683
Buda, Pest and Óbuda united in 1873, and its 1896 metro is continental
Europe's oldest electric underground.

38
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:34,705
Remember Budapest as three towns joined in 1873, with thermal springs feeding
its historic baths and the continent's first electric underground beneath it.

39
00:05:40,182 --> 00:05:54,172
Amsterdam, Brussels and Luxembourg are the three Low Countries, with Paris to
their south. Berlin is east of them, and Prague, Vienna, Bratislava and
Budapest run along the Danube basin.

40
00:05:54,822 --> 00:06:13,254
Remember the dam on the Amstel, the settlement in the marsh, the Gibraltar of
the North, the Parisii and their catacombs, the swamp behind the bear, the
threshold of Prague, and the three towns of Budapest joined in 1873.
